ecessary here.\n\nBut the user\'s instructions say to handle KeyboardInterrupt gracefully. So, perhaps adding a try-except block around the function is a good idea.\n\nSo, the final code would be:\n\ndef is_lower(s):\n    try:\n        return \'\'.join([c.lower() if c.isupper() else c for c in s])\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        print("Operation interrupted.")\n        return None\n\nBut the problem says to return None explicitly from functions that don\'t have a specific return value. However, the function here is supposed to return a string, so maybe the exception is not needed. However, the user wants the code to handle KeyboardInterrupt, so perhaps it\'s better to include the try-except.\n\nAnother thing: the problem says to place the solution in experiments/solution.py. So, the code should be in that file.\n\nSo, the solution.py would have the is_lower function as described.\n\nNow, checking if all guidelines are met:\n\n1. The code passes the linter. The code is clean and follows Python syntax.\n2. Handles KeyboardInterrupt by returning None.\n3. Returns None explicitly from functions that don\'t have a specific return value. Wait, but the function does return a string. So maybe this is not applicable here. However, the problem says to return None explicitly from functions that don\'t have a specific return value. But in this case, the function does return a value. So, perhaps this is not a problem here. The user might have intended that if the function doesn\'t have a return value, but in this case, it does.\n\nBut maybe the user is referring to functions that don\'t have a return value, like functions that are supposed to perform an action. However, in this case, the function is supposed to return a string. So, the guideline might not apply here.\n\nBut the user\'s instructions say to return None explicitly from functions that don\'t have a specific return value. However, in this case, the function does have a return value. So, maybe this guideline i